WEBVTT

00:00:00.705 --> 00:00:02.897
A photo is a grid of numbers.

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:06.098
This one is a hundred and twenty eight pixels
square:

00:00:06.118 --> 00:00:09.698
sixteen thousand, three hundred and eighty
four numbers.

00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:11.755
How few numbers could describe it?

00:00:11.865 --> 00:00:14.982
To find out, first watch what a matrix does to
a circle.

00:00:15.002 --> 00:00:17.890
Take this messy two by two matrix, M.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.190
It looks complicated, but it's three simple
moves.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.627
First, rotate by twenty degrees.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:27.777
Then stretch: three times along one axis, once
along the other.

00:00:27.797 --> 00:00:30.510
Then rotate again, by thirty degrees.

00:00:30.815 --> 00:00:32.445
Now apply M directly.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.707
The same ellipse!

00:00:33.727 --> 00:00:38.770
So M equals U, sigma, V transpose: rotate,
stretch, rotate.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.490
The ellipse's half axes, three and one, are
the singular values of M.

00:00:43.755 --> 00:00:47.672
A photo works the same way, with a hundred and
twenty eight stretches.

00:00:47.692 --> 00:00:51.885
Each one gives a layer: sigma, times a column,
times a row.

00:00:51.905 --> 00:00:53.697
Here is layer one alone.

00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:57.135
Every row is the same pattern, just brighter
or darker.

00:00:57.155 --> 00:00:59.105
And the stretches drop fast.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:01.752
Now add layers, largest first.

00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:05.002
How many until you recognise the cameraman and
his tripod?

00:01:05.022 --> 00:01:06.685
Pause and decide.

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:11.587
One layer is a plaid of stripes.

00:01:11.607 --> 00:01:13.312
Four, a dark figure.

00:01:13.332 --> 00:01:15.612
By eight, you can see the cameraman.

00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:19.675
By sixteen, his tripod too, from just a
quarter of the numbers.

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:22.770
And thirty two layers look almost like the
original.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.750
The small singular values hold what is left:
fine edges, grass texture, and noise.

00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:31.218
That's why low rank is everywhere.

00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:33.392
Compression keeps the top layers.

00:01:33.412 --> 00:01:35.530
Denoising drops the bottom ones:

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.405
the noise falls from thirty grey levels to
twenty, though some detail goes too.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:44.068
And large networks are fine tuned with low
rank adapters:

00:01:44.087 --> 00:01:46.530
a rank eight update to a big weight matrix

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.525
stores one number in two hundred and fifty
six.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.812
So remember three things.

00:01:51.832 --> 00:01:55.225
Every matrix is rotate, stretch, rotate.

00:01:55.245 --> 00:01:58.600
The singular values rank its directions by
importance.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.920
And keeping the top k layers gives the best
rank k approximation.

